# Bug: `if` written as a list element is not one element, and nothing says so.
#
# A list element is one atom, and the checker enforces that for an ordinary
# call: `[override_rule "*.rs"]` is refused with "a list element is one atom
# -- write `(override_rule ...)`". The same shape with `if` is not refused:
#
#   kanso run if_in_list_literal --interp   prints  [<fn> true "x" "y"]
#   kanso build if_in_list_literal          error: native backend: `if` as a
#                                           bare value is not yet supported
#
# Expected: the checker refuses `[if b "x" "y"]` with the same "one atom"
# diagnostic it gives a call, so both engines agree and the author is told
# to write `[(if b "x" "y")]`. Instead the interpreter silently builds a
# four-element list, and the native backend refuses the program with an
# error that names no file and no line.
import "./pick"

print "{pick/one true}"
